./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:199:Phase 2—Implement occurrence append with start-persistence gate: Before critic dispatch, call _append_tx with a STARTED event. On terminal persistence failure, emit PERSISTENCE_FAILED (not clean success). Append each accepted occurrence as an EXTERNAL_EFFECT_INTENT event with the serialized CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope in payload. Append reconciliation and disposition events after complete occurrence accounting validates. Only after all events for a revision are appended, construct and append a LedgerRevisionManifest event.
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:205:Phase 5—One-time M6 import: Traverse m6-corpus.json rounds v1-v5. For each round: map critique_custody.findings[] to CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope instances, evaluator_verdict.flag_verifications[] to FindingReconciliationEvent/DispositionEvent instances, plan-level faults/contract to LedgerRevisionManifest inputs. Classify every legacy record through LegacyClassification: has_authoritative_source → BACKFILLED; otherwise UNKNOWN or UNRECONSTRUCTABLE. Apply the partial-parse contract from failure-atomicity-table even though the corpus has zero failures. Reject unsupported schema versions via SUPPORTED_VERSIONS gate before any write. Import into the WBC store using the same atomic append path—each imported occurrence is an immutable event. Mark all imported records with metadata.source='m6-corpus-one-time-import'.
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:212:- [assume_and_proceed] The CL1 handoff has accepted_for_cl2: false with 5 procedural blockers (missing reviewer sign-off, M6 prerequisite INCOHERENT, proof-index FAILED, 4 ownership blockers, portfolio gate PC-SCOPE-BLOCKER-001). The CL2 constraint states 'Consume only an accepted CL1 handoff.' Can CL2 proceed against the frozen schemas and evidence while the handoff remains formally unaccepted? _(assumption: All CL1 must-gates pass (custody, freshness, replay equality, authority, schema, prerequisite). The five schemas are frozen with SHA-256 hashes in the handoff and independently verifiable from disk. The M6 corpus, manifest, and oracle are complete and hash-verified. The 5 blocking reasons are procedural/infrastructural—they do not invalidate the CL1 work product. CL2 proceeds conditionally, tracking the 5 blockers as open gates tracked in the CL2 handoff, with the understanding that schema hashes must not change and any blocker resolution that alters schemas would require CL2 scope recalibration.)_
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:214:- [assume_and_proceed] The WBC attempt_events table is attempt-scoped with PRIMARY KEY (attempt_id, sequence). The LedgerRevisionManifest spans multiple attempts and requires cross-attempt aggregation. No cross-attempt table or aggregation mechanism currently exists. Can CL2 implement this within the two-week scope without widening attempt_events? _(assumption: Implement a separate revision_manifests table in the same SQLite database (revision_hash TEXT PRIMARY KEY, prior_revision_hash, input_set_hash, domain_completeness, wbc_receipt_refs, event_ids, created_at_ns). The table is populated atomically in the same transaction as the final event of a revision using the shared store.conn. This avoids widening attempt_events and keeps the domain-specific aggregation separate from the generic event stream—consistent with the locked decision that projections carry no positive authority.)_
