./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1248:- CL1 handoff accepted_for_cl2 is false with 5 blocking reasons, but all CL1 must-gates pass (custody, freshness, replay equality, authority, schema, prerequisite). The semantic loop gate verdict is accepted:true with zero replay failures and zero-write assertion verified. The 5 blockers are procedural/infrastructural—missing reviewer sign-off, M6 prerequisite INCOHERENT (M5 attestation gap), proof-index downstream failure, 4 Run Authority ownership blockers, portfolio gate PC-SCOPE-BLOCKER-001—not material invalidations of the frozen schemas or evidence. (source: docs/critique-ledger/handoffs/cl1-contract-oracle.json:411-441, docs/critique-ledger/evidence/cl1-semantic-loop-gate.json:51-73; relevance: high)
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1250:- M6 corpus is complete (892KB, 5 rounds v1-v5, 12 artifact types per round) with overall=complete, zero missing_required_artifacts, zero unavailable_evidence. Oracle fact 6 confirms zero FAILED/DROPPED/MALFORMED instances across all rounds. The partial-parse contract is defined by the failure-atomicity-table: ParseStatus.FAILED records attempt but emits no findings; MALFORMED preserves evidence as UNAVAILABLE with reopen condition; TOMBSTONED marks record removed. LegacyClassification enum (BACKFILLED/UNKNOWN/CORRUPT/UNRECONSTRUCTABLE) enforces forward-fix-only—only has_authoritative_source permits BACKFILLED. (source: docs/critique-ledger/evidence/m6-corpus-manifest.json, docs/critique-ledger/evidence/m6-oracle.json:72-86, docs/critique-ledger/evidence/failure-atomicity-table.json, arnold/workflow/ledger_migrations.py:190-270; relevance: high)
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1269:| docs/critique-ledger/handoffs/cl1-contract-oracle.json | - | Authoritative CL1 handoff with schema hashes, owner/cutover mappings, WBC merge references, custody contracts, retention/atomicity decisions, and the machine-checkable accepted_for_cl2 derivation. CL2 must validate this handoff's hashes at start and reject if stale. The 5 blocking reasons are documented in open_gates. |
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1282:- Start persistence precedes critic dispatch; terminal persistence failure is visible persistence_failed|indeterminate, never clean success. The failure-atomicity-table governs all 13 failure modes.
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1283:- Occurrences are never merged away. Semantic identity and occurrence identity remain distinct. Projections are rebuildable and carry no positive authority.
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1287:- No new database service, event bus, authority plane, generalized knowledge store, embeddings, broad historical backfill, dual-write window, or old-reader retirement before CL5.
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1293:Phase 2—Implement occurrence append with start-persistence gate: Before critic dispatch, call _append_tx with a STARTED event. On terminal persistence failure, emit PERSISTENCE_FAILED (not clean success). Append each accepted occurrence as an EXTERNAL_EFFECT_INTENT event with the serialized CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope in payload. Append reconciliation and disposition events after complete occurrence accounting validates. Only after all events for a revision are appended, construct and append a LedgerRevisionManifest event.
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1299:Phase 5—One-time M6 import: Traverse m6-corpus.json rounds v1-v5. For each round: map critique_custody.findings[] to CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope instances, evaluator_verdict.flag_verifications[] to FindingReconciliationEvent/DispositionEvent instances, plan-level faults/contract to LedgerRevisionManifest inputs. Classify every legacy record through LegacyClassification: has_authoritative_source → BACKFILLED; otherwise UNKNOWN or UNRECONSTRUCTABLE. Apply the partial-parse contract from failure-atomicity-table even though the corpus has zero failures. Reject unsupported schema versions via SUPPORTED_VERSIONS gate before any write. Import into the WBC store using the same atomic append path—each imported occurrence is an immutable event. Mark all imported records with metadata.source='m6-corpus-one-time-import'.
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1306:- [assume_and_proceed] The CL1 handoff has accepted_for_cl2: false with 5 procedural blockers (missing reviewer sign-off, M6 prerequisite INCOHERENT, proof-index FAILED, 4 ownership blockers, portfolio gate PC-SCOPE-BLOCKER-001). The CL2 constraint states 'Consume only an accepted CL1 handoff.' Can CL2 proceed against the frozen schemas and evidence while the handoff remains formally unaccepted? _(assumption: All CL1 must-gates pass (custody, freshness, replay equality, authority, schema, prerequisite). The five schemas are frozen with SHA-256 hashes in the handoff and independently verifiable from disk. The M6 corpus, manifest, and oracle are complete and hash-verified. The 5 blocking reasons are procedural/infrastructural—they do not invalidate the CL1 work product. CL2 proceeds conditionally, tracking the 5 blockers as open gates tracked in the CL2 handoff, with the understanding that schema hashes must not change and any blocker resolution that alters schemas would require CL2 scope recalibration.)_
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:1308:- [assume_and_proceed] The WBC attempt_events table is attempt-scoped with PRIMARY KEY (attempt_id, sequence). The LedgerRevisionManifest spans multiple attempts and requires cross-attempt aggregation. No cross-attempt table or aggregation mechanism currently exists. Can CL2 implement this within the two-week scope without widening attempt_events? _(assumption: Implement a separate revision_manifests table in the same SQLite database (revision_hash TEXT PRIMARY KEY, prior_revision_hash, input_set_hash, domain_completeness, wbc_receipt_refs, event_ids, created_at_ns). The table is populated atomically in the same transaction as the final event of a revision using the shared store.conn. This avoids widening attempt_events and keeps the domain-specific aggregation separate from the generic event stream—consistent with the locked decision that projections carry no positive authority.)_
