.\"\"\""}]}
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2231:{"content": "     1|{\n     2|  \"schema\": \"cl.retention-class-mapping.v1\",\n     3|  \"generated_at\": \"2026-07-24T00:00:00Z\",\n     4|  \"description\": \"Maps every critique ledger evidence class to its retention, redaction, privacy, and encryption policy using only landed WBC enum values.\",\n     5|  \"evidence_class_mappings\": [\n     6|    {\n     7|      \"evidence_class\": \"critique_prompts_and_completions\",\n     8|      \"description\": \"Raw prompts sent to critique producer models and their completion outputs.\",\n     9|      \"retention_mode\": \"run\",\n    10|      \"redaction_mode\": \"default_on\",\n    11|      \"privacy_class\": \"confidential\",\n    12|      \"retention_class\": \"run\",\n    13|      \"encryption\": \"encrypted_governed_references\",\n    14|      \"access\": \"restricted to critique_ledger owner domain writers and compatibility readers\",\n    15|      \"availability\": \"RETAINED (redacted in fixture) / GOVERNED_REFERENCE (source repo)\",\n    16|      \"expiry\": \"run lifetime\",\n    17|      \"tombstone\": \"marker_tombstone on run completion\",\n    18|      \"reopen_behavior\": \"Reopen requires preserved repository at exact source revision\",\n    19|      \"rationale\": \"Prompts and completions may contain sensitive plan details and model interactions. Redacted on by default, encrypted at rest, governed references for audit trail.\"\n    20|    },\n    21|    {\n    22|      \"evidence_class\": \"critique_occurrence_envelopes\",\n    23|      \"description\": \"CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope records with occurrence_id, finding_id, semantic_finding_id, and producer metadata.\",\n    24|      \"retention_mode\": \"run\",\n    25|      \"redaction_mode\": \"default_on\",\n    26|      \"privacy_class\": \"confidential\",\n    27|      \"retention_class\": \"run\",\n    28|      \"encryption\": \"encrypted_governed_references\",\n    29|      \"access\": \"restricted to critique_ledger owner domain\",\n    30|      \"availability\": \"RETAINED\",\n    31|      \"expiry\": \"run lifetime\",\n    32|      \"tombstone\": \"marker_tombstone on run completion\",\n    33|      \"reopen_behavior\": \"Reopen requires accepted FindingReconciliationEvent from evaluator\",\n    34|      \"rationale\": \"Occurrence envelopes contain producer-local finding IDs and metadata that should not be exposed beyond the critique ledger domain.\"\n    35|    },\n    36|    {\n    37|      \"evidence_class\": \"finding_reconciliation_events\",\n    38|      \"description\": \"FindingReconciliationEvent records mapping occurrence IDs to canonical finding IDs with relationship types.\",\n    39|      \"retention_mode\": \"audit\",\n    40|      \"redaction_mode\": \"none\",\n    41|      \"privacy_class\": \"restricted\",\n    42|      \"retention_class\": \"audit\",\n    43|      \"encryption\": \"field_level_encryption\",\n    44|      \"access\": \"restricted access, audited reads\",\n    45|      \"availability\": \"RETAINED\",\n    46|      \"expiry\": \"audit lifetime (permanent until explicit tombstone)\",\n    47|      \"tombstone\": \"explicit authority tombstone required\",\n    48|      \"reopen_behavior\": \"Reopen requires new FindingReconciliationEvent from evaluator authority\",\n    49|      \"rationale\": \"Reconciliation decisions are the semantic authority — they must be preserved verbatim with no redaction to maintain the integrity of finding relationships. Field-level encryption protects sensitive fields while allowing structural audit.\"\n    50|    },\n    51|    {\n    52|      \"evidence_class\": \"finding_disposition_events\",\n    53|      \"description\": \"FindingDispositionEvent records across 8 disposition families with reason_subcodes, severity, evidence limits, action/non-action, and reopen predicates.\",\n    54|      \"retention_mode\": \"audit\",\n    55|      \"redaction_mode\": \"none\",\n    56|      \"privacy_class\": \"restricted\",\n    57|      \"retention_class\": \"audit\",\n    58|      \"encryption\": \"field_level_encryption\",\n    59|      \"access\": \"restricted access, audited reads\",\n    60|      \"availability\": \"RETAINED\",\n    61|", "total_lines": 143, "file_size": 8126, "truncated": true, "hint": "Use offset=61 to continue reading (showing 1-60 of 143 lines)", "is_binary": false, "is_image": false}
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2247:      "arguments": "{\"path\":\"/workspace/critique-ledger-m9-glm-20260723/Arnold/.megaplan/plans/cl2-wbc-backed-ledger-20260724-0351/plan.md\",\"content\":\"# Implementation Plan: CL2 — WBC-backed ledger persistence, import, and replay\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nImplement the frozen CL1 record contract through the existing WBC persistence\\nseams (`SqliteAttemptLedgerStore`, `SqliteLedgerOutbox`,\\n`SqliteLedgerPayloadStore`) so that critique occurrences, reconciliation\\nevents, disposition events, and ledger-revision manifests become durable,\\nidempotent, replayable WBC event-stream rows. Then add the one-time import of\\nthe retained M6 corpus, the read-only replay path, and a written CL2 handoff.\\n\\nRepository shape (verified):\\n\\n- `arnold/critique_ledger/schemas.py` — five frozen CL1 dataclasses at\\n  `cl.schema.v1` (`CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope`, `FindingReconciliationEvent`,\\n  `FindingDispositionEvent`, `DomainBriefingEnvelope`, `LedgerRevisionManifest`)\\n  with `to_dict`/`from_dict`. `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS={'cl.schema.v1'}` rejects\\n  other versions.\\n- `arnold/critique_ledger/semantic_loop.py` — pure deterministic replay:\\n  `validate_occurrence_custody`, `apply_reconciliation_events`,\\n  `apply_disposition_events`, `construct_manifest`, `build_briefing`,\\n  `project_reviser_input`, `project_gate_input`, `replay_full`.\\n- `arnold/workflow/attempt_ledger_store.py` — `SqliteAttemptLedgerStore`.\\n  `_EVENTS_TABLE_DDL` (`attempt_events`, PK `(attempt_id, sequence)`).\\n  `_append_tx` enforces the 4 Step-4 invariants (monotonic sequence,\\n  idempotency-key dedup wins over rejection, exactly-one-terminal,\\n  post-terminal rejection) in one `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`.\\n  `read_events`/`read_ledger`/`last_sequence`/`event_count`/`start_verified`/\\n  `terminal_or_indeterminate_verified` already exist.\\n- `arnold/workflow/ledger_outbox.py` — `append_event_with_outbox` proves the\\n  atomic event+outbox pattern on the shared `store.conn`.\\n- `arnold/workflow/ledger_payload_store.py` — shares `store.conn`, enforces the\\n  6 WBC payload boundaries.\\n- `arnold/workflow/execution_attempt_ledger.py` — `AttemptEventType` (11 generic\\n  values), `LedgerEvent` (`payload: dict | DurableRef | None`),\\n  `_LIFECYCLE_PRECEDENCE`, and `validate_event_ordering` (enforces precedence).\\n- `arnold/workflow/ledger_migrations.py` — `LegacyClassification` + evidence\\n  enforcement (forward-fix-only).\\n- `arnold/workflow/durable_refs.py` — `DurableRef` canonical shape.\\n- `docs/critique-ledger/evidence/retention-class-mapping.json` — occurrence =\\n  confidential/run; reconciliation/disposition = restricted/audit.\\n- `docs/critique-ledger/evidence/failure-atomicity-table.json` — the 13 failure\\n  modes + `forbidden_writes`.\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/m6-corpus.json` — 5-round import source.\\n\\nConstraints that govern every step:\\n\\n1. **CL1 frozen files must not change.** `test_cl1_handoff.py` recomputes and\\n   compares SHA-256 of `schemas.py`, `semantic_loop.py`, `critique_ledger/__init__.py`,\\n   `m6-corpus.json`, and the five `docs/critique-ledger/evidence/*.json` files.\\n   CL2 therefore adds **new modules** and **reuses `semantic_loop.replay_full`\\n   unchanged** for store-backed replay. It never edits frozen sources.\\n2. **No `AttemptEventType` widening, no `attempt_events` schema widening.**\\n   Critique records ride in `LedgerEvent.payload`; cross-attempt aggregation\\n   lives in a separate table on the same connection.\\n3. **Reuse, do not reimplement, WBC atomicity and policy.** Writes go through\\n   `_append_tx` / `append_event_with_outbox`; payloads go through\\n   `SqliteLedgerPayloadStore`.\\n\\n### Anchor note (flagged, not silently resolved)\\n\\nThe CL2 brief authorizes a *compatibility dual-write* of `faults.json` /\\ncustody artifacts \\\"during CL2\\\" with read-only cutover at CL5, while the Epic\\nNorth Star **anti-scope** forbids a \\\"dual-write period\\\". This plan treats the\\ntwo as reconcilable only because the ledger side is a **non-authoritative\\nmirror** (invariant: projections carry no positive authority) and `faults.json`\\nremains the sole authority for critique/gate/finalize consumers. Step 6 is\\ntherefore scoped as an *additive, guarded mirror*, not a shadow-authority\\ndual-write. If a reviewer reads the anti-scope as forbidding any mirror, Step 6\\nshould be deferred to CL5 and the rest of the plan is unaffected. This is a\\n**question for confirmation (Q2)**, not a silent narrowing.\\n\\n### Bridge decisions (deviation from the brief, evidence-based)\\n\\nThe brief's open-question assumption routes `FindingReconciliationEvent` →\\n`RECONCILIATION`. The code disagrees: `_LIFECYCLE_PRECEDENCE` (in\\n`execution_attempt_ledger.py:212-231`, enforced by `validate_event_ordering`\\nat `:1190-1268`) requires a preceding `PERSISTENCE_FAILED` before any\\n`RECONCILIATION` event. A normal critique stream has no `PERSISTENCE_FAILED`,\\nso that mapping makes the reconstructed ledger fail ordering validation.\\n\\nThis plan uses a precedence-safe mapping plus a **payload discriminator** so\\n`event_type` only signals the lifecycle role and the domain type rides in the\\npayload:\\n\\n| CL1 record | `AttemptEventType` | required predecessor (satisfied by) |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| `CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope` | `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_INTENT` | `STARTED` (we append STARTED first) |\\n| `FindingReconciliationEvent` | `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_OUTCOME` | `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_INTENT` (occurrences precede) |\\n| `FindingDispositionEvent` | `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_OUTCOME` | `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_INTENT` |\\n| `LedgerRevisionManifest` | `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_OUTCOME` | `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_INTENT` |\\n\\nThe payload dict carries `{\\\"cl_record_kind\\\": \\\"occurrence\\\"|\\\"reconciliation\\\"|\\\"disposition\\\"|\\\"manifest\\\", \\\"schema_version\\\": \\\"cl.schema.v1\\\", \\\"record\\\": <to_dict()>}`, so deserialization never relies on `event_type` alone. This resolves brief open-question #2 more robustly and is **question Q1** for confirmation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Phase 1: Foundation — serializer bridge (pure)\\n\\n### Step 1: Add the WBC bridge serializer (`arnold/critique_ledger/wbc_bridge.py`)\\n**Complexity: 3**\\n\\n1. **Add** a pure, side-effect-free serializer in a new module\\n   `arnold/critique_ledger/wbc_bridge.py`:\\n   - Constants: `CL_RECORD_KIND_OCCURRENCE = \\\"occurrence\\\"`, `_RECONCILIATION`,\\n     `_DISPOSITION`, `_MANIFEST`; the payload-discriminator contract above.\\n   - `envelope_to_event_payload(env: CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope) -> dict`\\n     (and the three siblings). Include `cl_record_kind` + `schema_version` +\\n     `record`.\\n   - `record_to_ledger_event(record, *, event_type, attempt_id, sequence,\\n     causal_predecessor_sequence, append_position, idempotency_key, occurred_at,\\n     observed_at, identity/provenance/adapter/versions/grant_ref) -> LedgerEvent`\\n     that selects the correct `AttemptEventType` per the mapping table and sets\\n     `payload` to the discriminated dict (or a `DurableRef` when evidence is a\\n     governed reference — see retention map).\\n   - `ledger_event_to_record(event: LedgerEvent)` inverse: inspect\\n     `payload[\\\"cl_record_kind\\\"]` and call the matching `from_dict`; raise a\\n     typed `CritiqueLedgerBridgeError` for unknown/missing discriminator or\\n     unsupported `schema_version` (fail closed).\\n   - `durable_ref_for(record_kind, digest, locator, store_id, ...) -> DurableRef`\\n     applying the retention-class mapping (occurrence →\\n     `PrivacyClass.CONFIDENTIAL`/`RetentionClass.RUN`; reconciliation/\\n     disposition/manifest → `PrivacyClass.RESTRICTED`/`RetentionClass.AUDIT`).\\n2. **Capture** the idempotency convention: the bridge derives a stable\\n   `idempotency_key` per logical record from `(attempt_id, cl_record_kind,\\n   record identity)` so a retry is a deterministic no-op.\\n3. **Test** round-trips for all four record kinds (`tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_wbc_bridge.py`):\\n   serialize → `LedgerEvent` → persist-via-in-memory-store → reload → deserialize\\n   equals original; discriminator round-trips; unsupported-version and\\n   missing-discriminator raise.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Phase 2: Persistence layer\\n\\n### Step 2: `CritiqueLedgerStore` writer with start gate, atomic append, and manifest publication (`arnold/critique_ledger/wbc_bridge.py`)\\n**Complexity: 4**\\n\\n1. **Add** `CritiqueLedgerStore`, a thin orchestrator constructed over an\\n   existing `SqliteAttemptLedgerStore` + `SqliteLedgerOutbox` +\\n   `SqliteLedgerPayloadStore` (all sharing `store.conn`). On construction it\\n   creates two new tables (idempotent `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`) on the same\\n   connection:\\n   - `revision_manifests(revision_hash TEXT PRIMARY KEY, manifest_id TEXT,\\n     prior_revision_hash TEXT, input_set_hash TEXT, domain_completeness TEXT,\\n     wbc_receipt_refs TEXT, event_ids TEXT, created_at_ns INTEGER)` —\\n     cross-attempt aggregation, separate from `attempt_events`.\\n   - `round_attempts(round_label TEXT, attempt_id TEXT, PRIMARY KEY(round_label,\\n     attempt_id))` — populated at occurrence-write time from\\n     `CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope.round_label`.\\n   Also `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_attempt_events_sequence ON\\n   attempt_events(sequence)` for cumulative cross-attempt ordered scans.\\n2. **Implement** the start-persistence gate: `begin_critique_attempt(...)` appends\\n   a `STARTED` event **before** any occurrence is written (per\\n   `failure-atomicity-table.json: start_persistence_failed`). On terminal\\n   persistence failure it appends `PERSISTENCE_FAILED`\\n   (`persistence_status=persistence_failed|indeterminate`), **never** clean success.\\n3. **Implement** occurrence append (`append_occurrence`): writes one\\n   `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_INTENT` event per accepted occurrence via\\n   `append_event_with_outbox` (atomic event + payload DurableRef + `round_attempts`\\n   row). `append_reconciliation` / `append_disposition` write\\n   `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_OUTCOME` events. All reuse `_append_tx` invariants.\\n4. **Implement** manifest publication `publish_revision(...)`: **only after\\n   complete occurrence accounting validates** (call a validation sub-step that\\n   mirrors `replay_full` custody + reconciliation-coverage checks; raise on\\n   `OCCURRENCE_UNMAPPED`/unresolved). In **one transaction** on `store.conn`:\\n   append the manifest `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_OUTCOME` event **and** insert the\\n   `revision_manifests` row, so a partial publication can never expose an\\n   accepted incomplete revision. `input_set_hash`/`prior_revision_hash` derived\\n   from the appended events.\\n5. **Test** (`test_wbc_bridge.py`): start-gate-before-occurrence; duplicate\\n   occurrence = idempotent no-op; post-terminal append rejected; manifest not\\n   published when reconciliation coverage incomplete; manifest+table insert are\\n   atomic (simulate failure between them leaves neither); partial publication\\n   cannot be read as an accepted revision.\\n\\n### Step 3: Replay indexes and the four read paths (`arnold/critique_ledger/wbc_bridge.py`)\\n**Complexity: 3**\\n\\n1. **Implement** read paths on `CritiqueLedgerStore`, all read-only and\\n   side-effect-free:\\n   - `read_attempt(attempt_id)` — per-attempt ordered events via existing PK\\n     scan (pattern a).\\n   - `read_round(round_label)` — join `round_attempts` → `attempt_events`\\n     (pattern b).\\n   - `read_domain(domain, domain_assignments)` — load relevant round/attempt\\n     events, deserialize, filter in memory by `semantic_finding_id → domain`\\n     using bounded `domain_assignments` (pattern c; no separate domain table\\n     needed since domain cardinality is bounded by briefing budgets).\\n   - `read_cumulative()` — ordered cross-attempt scan over\\n     `idx_attempt_events_sequence` (pattern d).\\n2. Each path returns deserialized CL1 records (occurrences/reconciliations/\\n   dispositions) via `ledger_event_to_record`, preserving occurrence identity\\n   distinct from semantic identity (occurrences are never merged away).\\n3. **Test** (`test_wbc_bridge.py`): all four paths return identical content for\\n   a single-round fixture; round/domain/cumulative agree with per-attempt\\n   concatenation; ordering is monotonic by sequence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Phase 3: Deterministic store-backed replay\\n\\n### Step 4: Store-backed replay reusing `replay_full`, plus store failure modes (`arnold/critique_ledger/store_replay.py`)\\n**Complexity: 3**\\n\\n1. **Add** `arnold/critique_ledger/store_replay.py` with\\n   `replay_from_store(store, *, round_label=None, domain=None,\\n   domain_assignments=None, ...) -> dict`. It deserializes the relevant records\\n   from the store via the Step-3 readers and calls **`semantic_loop.replay_full`\\n   unchanged** (no edits to the frozen module). Returns the same dict shape\\n   (`custody/reconciliation/disposition/manifest/briefing/projections`).\\n2. **Verify** byte-equivalence: replaying the same store twice yields identical\\n   projection hashes (reuse `canonical_hash` from `schemas.py`); store-backed\\n   replay matches an in-memory `replay_full` fed the same records.\\n3. **Extend** `tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_semantic_loop_failures.py`\\n   **additively** (new test class, do not modify existing pass-to-pass cases):\\n   store-backed failure modes — `PostTerminalAppendError`,\\n   `MonotonicSequenceError`, `persistence_failed` terminal, and partial-parse\\n   contract (`FAILED` records attempt but emits no findings; `MALFORMED` →\\n   `UNAVAILABLE` with reopen condition; `TOMBSTONED` marks removed).\\n4. **Assert** zero-write purity: `replay_from_store` performs no filesystem\\n   writes and no subprocess calls (mirrors `test_zero_write_mutation_gate`\\n   semantics). No model calls, no external effects.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Phase 4: One-time M6 import\\n\\n### Step 5: One-time importer for the retained M6 corpus (`arnold/critique_ledger/m6_importer.py`)\\n**Complexity: 4**\\n\\n1. **Add** `import_m6_corpus(store, *, corpus_path, target_schema_version)`\\n   that traverses `m6-corpus.json` rounds v1–v5. Before any write it validates\\n   `target_schema_version in SUPPORTED_VERSIONS` and **rejects** unsupported\\n   versions with **no partial publish** (write nothing).\\n2. **Map** each round: `critique_custody.findings[]` →\\n   `CritiqueOccurrenceEnvelope`; `evaluator_verdict.flag_verifications[]` →\\n   `FindingReconciliationEvent`/`FindingDispositionEvent`; plan-level\\n   faults/contract → `LedgerRevisionManifest` inputs. Apply the\\n   `failure-atomicity-table.json` partial-parse contract even though the corpus\\n   has zero failures.\\n3. **Classify** every legacy record through `ledger_migrations.LegacyClassification`\\n   + `ClassificationEvidence`: `has_authoritative_source=True` (the corpus is the\\n   authoritative retained source) → `BACKFILLED`; otherwise `UNKNOWN`/`UNRECONSTRUCTABLE`.\\n   Never synthesize `BACKFILLED` from circumstantial evidence\\n   (`ClassificationSafetyError`). Mark every imported record\\n   `metadata.source = \\\"m6-corpus-one-time-import\\\"` and surface semantic gaps as\\n   `unknown` (no invented duplicate/resolution/evidence/reopen relations).\\n4. **Import atomically and idempotently** through the same `CritiqueLedgerStore`\\n   append path (Step 2). Re-running import is a no-op via idempotency-key dedup;\\n   a crash mid-import leaves a valid, completable store (each occurrence is an\\n   immutable event).\\n5. **Test** (`tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_m6_importer.py`): all 5 rounds\\n   import; occurrence count matches corpus; re-import is a no-op; unsupported\\n   target version rejected with zero writes; `BACKFILLED` requires authoritative\\n   source; a deliberately corrupt/circumstantial-only record falls back to\\n   `UNKNOWN`/`UNRECONSTRUCTABLE`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Phase 5: Compatibility bridge and negative-test matrix\\n\\n### Step 6: Non-authoritative faults.json / custody mirror (`arnold/critique_ledger/flag_mirror.py`, hook in `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/orchestration/critique_custody.py`)\\n**Complexity: 3** — *gated by Q2; deferrable to CL5*\\n\\n1. **Add** `mirror_flags_to_ledger(store, flag_registry, *, round_label)`\\n   in a new `flag_mirror.py` that emits a non-authoritative\\n   `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_INTENT` event carrying a `FlagSyncEnvelope` payload\\n   (`cl_record_kind=\\\"flag_sync\\\"`). It **never** writes `faults.json` and carries\\n   **no positive authority** — it is rebuildable evidence only.\\n2. **Wire** a single additive, guarded call after `write_critique_clearance` /\\n   `update_flags_after_critique` in `critique_custody.py` so `faults.json`\\n   remains the authority and its existing write path is unchanged. The call must\\n   fail soft: a mirror failure must not break the active critique/gate path.\\n3. **Test** (`tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_flag_mirror.py` +\\n   `tests/orchestration/test_critique_custody.py`): `faults.json` content\\n   unchanged by the mirror; mirror event present and rebuildable; mirror failure\\n   does not corrupt the critique path.\\n\\n### Step 7: Fixture matrix (`tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_*.json`)\\n**Complexity: 2**\\n\\n1. **Create** the required fixtures: `cl2_current_source.json`,\\n   `cl2_target_only.json`, `cl2_future_version.json`, `cl2_corrupt.json`,\\n   `cl2_redacted.json`, `cl2_unavailable.json`, `cl2_duplicate.json`,\\n   `cl2_out_of_order.json`, `cl2_concurrent.json`, `cl2_crash.json`.\\n2. Each fixture is a minimal, hand-checkable input exercising one\\n   import/replay/failure boundary. Future-version and corrupt fixtures must be\\n   rejected by the version/discriminator gates; duplicate and out-of-order must\\n   be handled by idempotency/monotonicity; redacted/unavailable must surface as\\n   `unknown` without synthesis.\\n\\n### Step 8: Negative tests — replay/persistence cannot mutate external state (`tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl2_negative.py`)\\n**Complexity: 3**\\n\\n1. **Prove** ledger/replay cannot mutate plan, gate, lifecycle, queue,\\n   Git/provider, delivery, or external-effect state: snapshot relevant artifacts\\n   before/after each store operation and assert byte-equality; assert no\\n   subprocess/network/filesystem-mutation outside the SQLite DB.\\n2. **Prove** duplicate replay is a no-op (idempotency-key dedup returns the\\n   existing event; event count unchanged).\\n3. **Prove** partial publication cannot expose an accepted incomplete revision\\n   (no `revision_manifests` row until accounting validates; readers never return\\n   a manifest for an incomplete revision).\\n4. **Prove** the version gate and discriminator fail closed (future-version and\\n   corrupt fixtures rejected with zero writes).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Phase 6: Handoff\\n\\n### Step 9: Write and validate the CL2 handoff (`docs/critique-ledger/handoffs/cl2-ledger-replay.json`)\\n**Complexity: 2**\\n\\n1. **Write** `cl2-ledger-replay.json` (schema `cl.handoff.v1`) with: API/schema\\n   hashes for the new modules; backend/atomicity decision (shared `store.conn`\\n   WAL + `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`); the event-type mapping + discriminator contract;\\n   one-time import map (5 rounds, classifications); replay hashes\\n   (in-memory vs store-backed); fixture index; freshness rules; fault/failure\\n   results; backup/restore prerequisites; the **5 CL1 open gates tracked as\\n   open** (reviewer sign-off, M6 prerequisite, proof-index, ownership, portfolio\\n   gate); and unresolved limits (compatibility mirror pending CL5 cutover).\\n2. **Test** (`tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl2_handoff.py`): handoff is\\n   valid JSON, declares `cl.handoff.v1`, recomputable API/schema hashes match\\n   disk, replay hashes match a live replay, fixture index matches on-disk\\n   fixtures, and the 5 CL1 blockers are recorded as open gates.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Execution Order\\n\\n1. Step 1 (serializer) — pure foundation, no I/O; unblocks everything.\\n2. Steps 2–3 (writer + readers) — the persistence core.\\n3. Step 4 (store replay) — depends on Steps 1–3.\\n4. Step 5 (importer) — depends on Step 2.\\n5. Step 7 (fixtures) — cheap, enables Steps 5/8; can run in parallel with 4/5.\\n6. Step 8 (negative tests) — depends on 2–4 + 7.\\n7. Step 6 (flag mirror) — *gated by Q2*; lowest coupling, do near the end so any\\n   revert does not affect the persistence/replay/import deliverable.\\n8. Step 9 (handoff) — last, after all hashes are stable.\\n\\n## Validation Order\\n\\n1. `test_wbc_bridge.py` (serializer + writer + readers) — cheapest, most targeted.\\n2. `test_store_replay.py` + the additive `test_semantic_loop_failures` cases.\\n3. `test_m6_importer.py` against the real `m6-corpus.json` fixture.\\n4. `test_cl2_negative.py` over the fixture matrix.\\n5. The **existing pass-to-pass floor** must stay green throughout and is the\\n   final gate: `test_schemas`, `test_semantic_loop`, `test_semantic_loop_failures`,\\n   `test_m6_corpus`, `test_policy_tables`, `test_cl1_handoff`,\\n   `test_zero_write_mutation_gate`, plus the WBC store/outbox/payload suites.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Success criteria\\n\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"All existing pass_to_pass suites remain green: test_schemas, test_semantic_loop, test_semantic_loop_failures, test_m6_corpus, test_policy_tables, test_cl1_handoff, test_zero_write_mutation_gate, test_attempt_ledger_store, test_ledger_outbox.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"CL1 frozen-file hashes are unchanged (schemas.py, semantic_loop.py, critique_ledger/__init__.py, m6-corpus.json, and the five docs/critique-ledger/evidence/*.json) — equivalently test_cl1_handoff still passes.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"The bridge serializer round-trips occurrence/reconciliation/disposition/manifest through LedgerEvent.payload with a cl_record_kind discriminator, and deserialization reconstructs the typed CL1 record (unsupported schema_version / missing discriminator fail closed).\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"Start persistence precedes occurrence append; terminal persistence failure is visible as persistence_failed|indeterminate, never clean success.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"Every accepted producer occurrence appears once; a duplicate append is an idempotent no-op (event count unchanged).\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"A LedgerRevisionManifest is published only after complete occurrence accounting validates, and the manifest event + revision_manifests row land atomically; a partial publication can never be read as an accepted revision.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"Store-backed replay (read-only, no model calls / no subprocess / no filesystem writes) reproduces replay_full projections byte-identically; replaying twice yields identical hashes.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"The one-time M6 importer imports all 5 rounds, is idempotent on re-run, rejects unsupported target versions with zero partial writes, marks semantic gaps unknown, and only uses BACKFILLED from authoritative source evidence.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"Negative tests prove ledger/replay cannot mutate plan, gate, lifecycle, queue, Git/provider, delivery, or external-effect state.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"faults.json remains writable by critique/gate/finalize and its content is unchanged by any ledger mirror; the ledger mirror carries no positive authority.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"cl2-ledger-replay.json handoff is valid, its API/schema hashes recompute from disk, its replay hashes match a live replay, and the 5 CL1 blockers are recorded as open gates.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"must\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"No new AttemptEventType values are added and the attempt_events table DDL is unchanged; cross-attempt aggregation uses a separate revision_manifests table.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"should\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"run_tests\\\", \\\"read_files\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"New source modules are focused (each well under ~400 lines) and the serializer/store/replay layers are cleanly separated.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"should\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"read_files\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"Domain replay uses bounded in-memory filtering via domain_assignments rather than a separate domain_attempts table.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"info\\\", \\\"requires\\\": [\\\"read_files\\\"]}\\n- {\\\"criterion\\\": \\\"The 5 CL1 procedural blockers (reviewer sign-off, M6 prerequisite, proof-index, ownership, portfolio gate) remain resolvable out-of-band; their resolution must not alter frozen CL1 hashes or it triggers CL2 replan.\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"info\\\", \\\"requires\\\": []}\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Questions (material)\\n\\n- **Q1 (event mapping):** Confirm the precedence-safe mapping (occurrence = `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_INTENT`; reconciliation/disposition/manifest = `EXTERNAL_EFFECT_OUTCOME`) with a `cl_record_kind` payload discriminator, instead of the brief's `RECONCILIATION` mapping. The code requires a preceding `PERSISTENCE_FAILED` for `RECONCILIATION` (`validate_event_ordering`), which the brief's mapping would violate. This changes the bridge contract and the discriminator format.\\n- **Q2 (dual-write scope):** Should the non-authoritative faults.json/custody mirror (Step 6) land in this milestone, or be deferred to CL5? It sits in tension with the Epic anti-scope \\\"no dual-write period\\\" — safe only as a non-authoritative mirror with `faults.json` remaining sole authority. If deferred, the rest of the plan is unaffected.\\n- **Q3 (attempt topology):** Are evaluator reconciliation/disposition/manifest records written into the **producer attempt's** stream or a **separate evaluator attempt** stream? This affects `round_attempts` population and manifest cross-attempt aggregation. Plan assumes the writer accepts an explicit `attempt_id` per write so either topology is supported; confirm the intended default.\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n- CL1 frozen sources are **not edited**; store replay reuses `semantic_loop.replay_full` unchanged, so frozen hashes stay valid.\\n- WBC atomicity is reused as-is (`_append_tx`, `append_event_with_outbox`, shared `store.conn`); no new transaction mechanism, no new DB service, no event bus.\\n- `revision_manifests` and `round_attempts` are new tables on the same SQLite DB; `idx_attempt_events_sequence` is added; `attempt_events` DDL is untouched.\\n- DurableRef retention follows `retention-class-mapping.json` (occurrence = confidential/run; reconciliation/disposition/manifest = restricted/audit).\\n- CL2 proceeds **conditionally** against frozen CL1 schemas/evidence; the 5 CL1 blockers are tracked as open gates in the CL2 handoff. Any blocker resolution that alters frozen hashes triggers CL2 replan.\\n- The importer classifies the M6 corpus as `BACKFILLED` only because the corpus is the authoritative retained source; all other gaps are `UNKNOWN`/`UNRECONSTRUCTABLE`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## changed_surfaces\\n\\nSource (new):\\n- `arnold/critique_ledger/wbc_bridge.py`\\n- `arnold/critique_ledger/store_replay.py`\\n- `arnold/critique_ledger/m6_importer.py`\\n- `arnold/critique_ledger/flag_mirror.py` *(gated by Q2)*\\n\\nSource (modified — additive only):\\n- `arnold_pipelines/megaplan/orchestration/critique_custody.py` *(single guarded mirror hook; gated by Q2)*\\n\\nTests (new):\\n- `tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_wbc_bridge.py`\\n- `tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_store_replay.py`\\n- `tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_m6_importer.py`\\n- `tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl2_negative.py`\\n- `tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl2_handoff.py`\\n- `tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_flag_mirror.py` *(gated by Q2)*\\n\\nTests (modified — additive only):\\n- `tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_semantic_loop_failures.py` *(new store-backed failure test class; existing cases untouched)*\\n\\nFixtures (new):\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_current_source.json`\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_target_only.json`\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_future_version.json`\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_corrupt.json`\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_redacted.json`\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_unavailable.json`\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_duplicate.json`\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_out_of_order.json`\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_concurrent.json`\\n- `tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/cl2_crash.json`\\n\\nDocs (new):\\n- `docs/critique-ledger/handoffs/cl2-ledger-replay.json`\\n\\nExplicitly NOT changed (CL1-frozen): `arnold/critique_ledger/schemas.py`,\\n`arnold/critique_ledger/semantic_loop.py`, `arnold/critique_ledger/__init__.py`,\\n`tests/fixtures/critique_ledger/m6-corpus.json`, and the five\\n`docs/critique-ledger/evidence/*.json`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## test_blast_radius\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"strategy\\\": \\\"scoped\\\",\\n  \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"high\\\",\\n  \\\"selectors\\\": [\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_wbc_bridge.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Covers the new serializer + writer + readers (the core changed surface).\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_store_replay.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Covers store-backed replay byte-equivalence and zero-write purity.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_m6_importer.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Covers the one-time import against the real m6-corpus fixture.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl2_negative.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Covers the no-mutation / idempotency / partial-publication invariants over the fixture matrix.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl2_handoff.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Validates the written CL2 handoff hashes.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_semantic_loop_failures.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Existing suite plus additive store-backed failure cases; must stay green.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_semantic_loop.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Frozen replay regression floor.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl1_handoff.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Proves CL1 frozen hashes are unchanged.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_schemas.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Frozen schema regression floor.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_m6_corpus.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Import-source completeness regression floor.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_policy_tables.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Atomicity/privacy policy regression floor.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold_pipelines/megaplan/test_zero_write_mutation_gate.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Zero-write purity gate the store replay must honor.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/workflow/test_attempt_ledger_store.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"WBC store primitives reused by the bridge; regression floor.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/arnold/workflow/test_ledger_outbox.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Atomic outbox pattern reused for manifest publication.\\\"},\\n    {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"path\\\", \\\"value\\\": \\\"tests/orchestration/test_critique_custody.py\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Touched by the optional guarded mirror hook.\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"changed_surfaces\\\": [\\n    \\\"arnold/critique_ledger/wbc_bridge.py\\\",\\n    \\\"arnold/critique_ledger/store_replay.py\\\",\\n    \\\"arnold/critique_ledger/m6_importer.py\\\",\\n    \\\"arnold/critique_ledger/flag_mirror.py\\\",\\n    \\\"arnold_pipelines/megaplan/orchestration/critique_custody.py\\\",\\n    \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_wbc_bridge.py\\\",\\n    \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_store_replay.py\\\",\\n    \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_m6_importer.py\\\",\\n    \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl2_negative.py\\\",\\n    \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl2_handoff.py\\\",\\n    \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_flag_mirror.py\\\",\\n    \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_semantic_loop_failures.py\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"always_run\\\": [\\n    \\\"tests/arnold/critique_ledger/test_cl1_handoff.py\\\",\\n    \\\"tests/arnold_pipelines/megaplan/test_zero_write_mutation_gate.py\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"full_suite_fallback\\\": true,\\n  \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"CL2 is almost entirely additive new modules reusing frozen CL1 schemas and landed WBC primitives. The scoped set covers every new module/fixture, the additive failure-mode extension, and the full existing critique-ledger + WBC regression floor (frozen hashes, replay equality, policy tables, zero-write). The critique_custody test is included because of the optional guarded mirror hook; full-suite fallback guarantees no silent regression in the broader megaplan path.\\\",\\n  \\\"import_graph\\\": {\\n    \\\"degraded\\\": false,\\n    \\\"dependent_tests\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"unresolved\\\": []\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\"}"
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2272:The brief authorizes a faults.json/custody "dual-write" while the Epic anti-scope forbids a "dual-write period." I scoped Step 6 as a **non-authoritative mirror** (ledger carries no positive authority; `faults.json` stays sole authority) and marked it **deferrable to CL5** if a reviewer reads the anti-scope strictly (**Q2**).
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2310:The evaluator remains the semantic routing and reconciliation authority. WBC
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2312:the critique ledger never becomes an execution authority, lifecycle writer,
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2346:    lifecycle authority. The migration switches the complete critique loop at
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2368:  hard-coded similarity threshold as semantic authority.
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2369:- No parallel execution ledger, authority plane, transition writer, repair
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2373:- No canary, prolonged shadow authority, dual-write period, broad mixed-version
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2400:- CL1 handoff accepted_for_cl2 is false with 5 blocking reasons, but all CL1 must-gates pass (custody, freshness, replay equality, authority, schema, prerequisite). The semantic loop gate verdict is accepted:true with zero replay failures and zero-write assertion verified. The 5 blockers are procedural/infrastructural—missing reviewer sign-off, M6 prerequisite INCOHERENT (M5 attestation gap), proof-index downstream failure, 4 Run Authority ownership blockers, portfolio gate PC-SCOPE-BLOCKER-001—not material invalidations of the frozen schemas or evidence. (source: docs/critique-ledger/handoffs/cl1-contract-oracle.json:411-441, docs/critique-ledger/evidence/cl1-semantic-loop-gate.json:51-73; relevance: high)
./critique-ledger-m9-planner-prompt.txt:2402:- M6 corpus is complete (892KB, 5 rounds v1-v5, 12 artifact types per round) with overall=complete, zero missing_required_artifacts, zero unavailable_evidence. Oracle fact 6 confirms zero FAILED/DROPPED/MALFORMED instances across all rounds. The partial-parse contract is defined by the failure-atomicity-table: ParseStatus.FAILED records attempt but emits no findings; MALFORMED preserves evidence as UNAVAILABLE with reopen condition; TOMBSTONED marks record removed. LegacyClassification enum (BACKFILLED/UNKNOWN/CORRUPT/UNRECONSTRUCTABLE) enforces forward-fix-only—only has_authoritative_source permits BACKFILLED. (source: docs/critique-ledger/evidence/m6-corpus-manifest.json, docs/critique-ledger/evidence/m6-oracle.json:72-86, docs/critique-ledger/evidence/failure-atomicity-table.json, arnold/workflow/ledger_migrations.py:190-270; relevance: high)
